lesson g6-solving-equations v=1 format=teach grade=6 unit=6 subject=maths levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Solving equations with a letter"
  goal "Write an equation for a story and solve it by doing the same to both sides, including equations with brackets to multiply out and with x on both sides, then check the answer in the equation."

  look {
    scene 35x13 {
      balanceeq scales left="3x + 5" right="26" at=canvas(1, 1)
      waterwheel wheel paddles=8 lit=0 right-of=scales gap=1
    }
    say """
      Three sacks of x kg and a 5 kg weight balance 26 kg at the mill. Take 5 kg off both pans and
      the balance still holds: 3x = 21. Share both pans into three and x = 7. Undo the steps in the
      opposite order to the one they were done in, and do the same to both sides each time. Then
      check: 3 × 7 + 5 is 26.
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked algebra.gorge-two-step f=0 a=3 b=5 x=7 level=medium
    }
    practice algebra.flour-sacks-one-step count=1 seed=3
    show algebra.gorge-two-step f=1 a=3 b=7 x=9 easy-f=0 easy-a=2 easy-b=9 easy-x=5 hard-f=1 hard-a=4 hard-b=7 hard-x=4.5
    practice algebra.miller-sacks-story count=1 seed=11
    practice algebra.which-equation-fits count=1 seed=2
    practice algebra.wheel-brackets count=1 seed=6
    practice algebra.bridge-sections count=1 seed=4
    practice algebra.same-solution count=1 seed=7
    practice algebra.bridge-both-sides count=1 seed=9
    level hard {
      practice algebra.gorge-two-step count=1 seed=8
    }
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show algebra.divide-first-mistake a=3 k=2 x=7 easy-a=2 easy-x=6 hard-a=4 hard-x=10
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show algebra.cart-loads v=3 easy-v=1 hard-v=5
  }
  remember {
    say "Do the same to both sides, undoing the last step first. Then put the answer back in to check it."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The balance is the picture to hold on to: whatever comes off one pan comes off the other, so it
    stays level. Solving undoes the steps in reverse, the adding or taking away first and then the
    times or the share. The common slip is to share first and share only part of a side, which is
    Leo's mistake in the two-star question; putting his answer back in shows it at once, which is
    why checking by substituting is part of the goal. In the bill question the child writes the
    equation, so ask what x stands for before anything is written. The rope bridge needs the
    brackets multiplied out, every term inside them, and the x terms collected, before the two
    steps are undone. With x on both pans, take the same number of x off both first. The second
    reasoning questions ask which equation fits a story and which keeps the balance, without
    solving. The three-star question has many answers to the equation but only a few in whole
    sacks: trying one oat sack, then two, then three, is the method. If two steps are still hard,
    stay here another day with one-step equations on the balance. Off the page, think of a number,
    double it and add 3, and have the child find the number from the result.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level the one-step equations multiply, 3x + 5 = 26 is worked in the teacher's pen, the two-step equations only add, the bill is for 3 or 4 sacks, the story shares between 2 carts, the brackets hold 2 or 3 lots, the bridge has two long sections, the same-solution choice has doubling at A, x on both sides leaves one x, Leo shares by 2, and the cart takes 30 kg in 2 kg and 5 kg sacks."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the one-step equations share, two of the two-step equations have a half in the answer, the bill is for 5 or 6 sacks, the story shares between 4 carts, the brackets hold 4 or 5 lots, the bridge has three or four long sections, x on both sides leaves several x, Leo shares by 4, and the cart takes 30 kg in 2 kg and 3 kg sacks, with four loads to find."
  }
}
